WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.097 --> 00:00:03.492
- That meal was 2,000
milligrams of sodium.

00:00:03.492 --> 00:00:06.193
- Flashes of just, heat.

00:00:06.193 --> 00:00:07.598
- No, I hate throwing up.

00:00:07.598 --> 00:00:11.265
(dramatic orchestral music)

00:00:12.237 --> 00:00:13.070
- Damn.

00:00:15.582 --> 00:00:17.543
- [Maggie] Being prepared for disasters

00:00:17.543 --> 00:00:18.847
is something that is important for people

00:00:18.847 --> 00:00:20.146
across the globe.

00:00:20.146 --> 00:00:22.468
The American Red Cross and FEMA recommend

00:00:22.468 --> 00:00:24.299
putting together a kit of supplies

00:00:24.299 --> 00:00:26.542
that last at least 72 hours.

00:00:26.542 --> 00:00:30.121
This includes non-perishable,
easy to prepare food.

00:00:30.121 --> 00:00:32.359
We tried to make it on
one of these three-day

00:00:32.359 --> 00:00:33.942
survival food kits.

00:00:34.962 --> 00:00:36.633
- So we're gonna be doing the 72-hour

00:00:36.633 --> 00:00:38.260
survival food challenge.

00:00:38.260 --> 00:00:39.359
- For the next three
days, we're only gonna be

00:00:39.359 --> 00:00:41.357
eating emergency food.

00:00:41.357 --> 00:00:43.308
- And it's only supposed
to last three days,

00:00:43.308 --> 00:00:45.143
in the hopes that someone
will come save us.

00:00:45.143 --> 00:00:49.310
- I think I have a little
bit of survivalist in me.

00:00:50.546 --> 00:00:53.106
- I'm the kind of person who
always wants to be prepared.

00:00:53.106 --> 00:00:56.075
- I can weave rope, but I don't have

00:00:56.075 --> 00:00:57.379
emergency food supply.

00:00:57.379 --> 00:00:58.726
- Especially we're in California,

00:00:58.726 --> 00:01:00.888
so there's gonna be and earthquake soon,

00:01:00.888 --> 00:01:01.745
it sounds like.

00:01:01.745 --> 00:01:03.850
And there's just disasters
happening everywhere.

00:01:03.850 --> 00:01:05.683
I just wanna be ready.

00:01:06.594 --> 00:01:08.019
- It looks like easy-mac.

00:01:08.019 --> 00:01:10.379
Carefully add two cups of boiling water.

00:01:10.379 --> 00:01:12.211
I have to wait eight to nine minutes,

00:01:12.211 --> 00:01:13.635
but I think I'm gonna eat it in four.

00:01:13.635 --> 00:01:17.705
- It smells pretty home-cooked, I think.

00:01:17.705 --> 00:01:18.705
- I approve.

00:01:19.779 --> 00:01:20.988
I like it a lot.

00:01:20.988 --> 00:01:23.821
This is great. I'm really excited.

00:01:25.434 --> 00:01:27.390
Welcome back to Cooking
in Bags with Maggie.

00:01:27.390 --> 00:01:28.811
- This is my dinner today.

00:01:28.811 --> 00:01:30.316
- This is an old family recipe.

00:01:30.316 --> 00:01:34.432
- It reminds me of my old
college cafeteria food.

00:01:34.432 --> 00:01:36.021
- Really freakin' good.

00:01:36.021 --> 00:01:37.650
- How is it?

00:01:37.650 --> 00:01:38.483
- I like it.

00:01:38.483 --> 00:01:40.766
- Okay, but I do need
it back for my calories.

00:01:40.766 --> 00:01:41.599
Come on.

00:01:46.124 --> 00:01:48.251
- This is actually really good.

00:01:48.251 --> 00:01:49.918
- Just a little bit.

00:01:50.821 --> 00:01:52.162
That's so good!

00:01:52.162 --> 00:01:53.995
It's lunch time.

00:01:53.995 --> 00:01:55.744
The beef stroganoff.

00:01:55.744 --> 00:01:57.335
I don't know what stroganoff is,

00:01:57.335 --> 00:01:58.918
but it sounds good.

00:01:59.980 --> 00:02:00.897
Lunch time,

00:02:01.855 --> 00:02:05.438
lunch time was where
things went all wrong.

00:02:07.961 --> 00:02:10.977
This one is pretty high in sodium.

00:02:10.977 --> 00:02:14.394
So, as I'm finishing the beef stroganoff,

00:02:14.394 --> 00:02:16.388
I'm hitting a wall where it just

00:02:16.388 --> 00:02:17.888
feels very creamy.

00:02:19.936 --> 00:02:21.684
My chest is like, uncomfortable,

00:02:21.684 --> 00:02:23.392
in like, this area.

00:02:23.392 --> 00:02:25.496
And my body just like,

00:02:25.496 --> 00:02:27.329
went into like a weird

00:02:28.559 --> 00:02:29.392
shock.

00:02:29.392 --> 00:02:31.207
I don't wanna throw up.

00:02:31.207 --> 00:02:33.124
No, I hate throwing up.

00:02:34.058 --> 00:02:36.134
I wonder how Maggie's doing.

00:02:36.134 --> 00:02:38.579
I wonder if she's had
the beef stroganoff yet.

00:02:38.579 --> 00:02:40.572
- Like, Kane messaged me, and was like,

00:02:40.572 --> 00:02:42.161
"Hey, how are you doing? Have you had

00:02:42.161 --> 00:02:43.629
"the beef stroganoff yet?"

00:02:43.629 --> 00:02:45.256
And I was like, "Oh yeah."

00:02:45.256 --> 00:02:46.355
I'm sweating.

00:02:46.355 --> 00:02:48.589
I don't know if it's the heat,

00:02:48.589 --> 00:02:52.378
or if it's the salt
coursing through my veins.

00:02:52.378 --> 00:02:53.437
He goes like, "How do you feel?"

00:02:53.437 --> 00:02:54.944
I was like, "Weird."

00:02:54.944 --> 00:02:56.027
I feel weird.

00:03:01.753 --> 00:03:04.604
It's been a rough couple of days.

00:03:04.604 --> 00:03:07.454
Three days, two and a half?

00:03:07.454 --> 00:03:10.223
I think it's just the
sodium that's getting to me.

00:03:10.223 --> 00:03:12.344
Really want fresh food right now.

00:03:12.344 --> 00:03:15.794
I just, fresh food, nothing
freeze-dried, please.

00:03:15.794 --> 00:03:17.102
- [Co-Worker] You ate the
whole bag, Kane, one meal?

00:03:17.102 --> 00:03:18.574
- Yeah, I've been eating the whole

00:03:18.574 --> 00:03:20.456
bag of all of this.

00:03:20.456 --> 00:03:21.435
- Kane.

00:03:21.435 --> 00:03:23.343
- You've got, that means you had,

00:03:23.343 --> 00:03:24.731
I mean, if you ate this, that would mean,

00:03:24.731 --> 00:03:25.956
what's eight times three, 24?

00:03:25.956 --> 00:03:28.117
2400 milligrams of sodium.

00:03:28.117 --> 00:03:28.950
- Okay, you're gonna kill him.

00:03:28.950 --> 00:03:32.025
- On the last day, I
wanted to finish strong.

00:03:32.025 --> 00:03:34.791
For lunch, I heated up
the pasta primavera.

00:03:34.791 --> 00:03:36.261
I ate half of it.

00:03:36.261 --> 00:03:38.540
And then I just could not eat anymore.

00:03:38.540 --> 00:03:40.925
Looked in the fridge and like, grabbed a

00:03:40.925 --> 00:03:42.679
like, the little bell pepper things,

00:03:42.679 --> 00:03:44.184
where you can just snack on.

00:03:44.184 --> 00:03:47.034
The juiciest thing I've ever eaten.

00:03:47.034 --> 00:03:48.785
Like, I came alive from eating that.

00:03:48.785 --> 00:03:52.202
- I could eat a whole head of lettuce.

00:03:52.202 --> 00:03:53.421
I just need something fresh.

00:03:53.421 --> 00:03:56.066
- I feel like I still
will get some of these

00:03:56.066 --> 00:03:58.952
for my emergency pack, for when I need it.

00:03:58.952 --> 00:04:00.377
Because at the end of the day,

00:04:00.377 --> 00:04:02.533
we struggled so much with this because

00:04:02.533 --> 00:04:04.932
we're not in an emergency situation.

00:04:04.932 --> 00:04:09.165
- I still have shelter
and clothes and internet.

00:04:09.165 --> 00:04:11.081
The only thing I'm having
to deal with right now

00:04:11.081 --> 00:04:12.385
is just the food.

00:04:12.385 --> 00:04:17.222
- In emergency situations,
what we initially see,

00:04:17.222 --> 00:04:18.892
there's like, probably ten more issues

00:04:18.892 --> 00:04:20.402
that people are dealing with.

00:04:20.402 --> 00:04:24.350
- This made me realize that
I'm in a place right now,

00:04:24.350 --> 00:04:26.301
where I can actually help.

00:04:26.301 --> 00:04:29.595
I'm gonna donate some money to help.

00:04:29.595 --> 00:04:32.164
It should be something
that I do more often.

00:04:32.164 --> 00:04:35.081
(upbeat jazz trio)

00:04:38.067 --> 00:04:40.591
(singing) ♫ Carry On

00:04:40.591 --> 00:04:43.032
♫ But you can have a bag day

00:04:43.032 --> 00:04:44.985
♫ You're takin' one down

00:04:44.985 --> 00:04:47.902
♫ You sing a sad--

